 #:seon.task{:name "a-chain-branch-write-wakes-root-or-says-it-does-not"
             :plan "S5 run 2 hand act 2 (`docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/s5-run-2.md#Run`, Broken item 4): after the D3 repair, a branch transaction on `:chain-f1f6dc8e6652` setting the first task `:running` with `:seon.task/next`, no attempt started for 37 s until root offered `:seon.flow/plan-prime` on the cluster's `:seon.cluster.wake/channel` by hand. TASK-CHAIN section 5 answers whether this is a gap: `seon.cluster.wake/route!` wakes root's plan channel only for added datoms of starting attributes (`:seon.wake/starts`) on main (an attempt end is one, the chain fact's installation is one, `my.root/run!` offers its own wake), so a write on a chain branch is not expected to wake root. Decision recorded: expected, not a defect by itself; the hand act existed only because D3 (`seon.task/chain-of-a-filed-task-runs-its-first-task`, lane s5-d3) left the first task unstarted, and a correct `seon.task/chain!` makes the chain fact's own wake sufficient. Done when `seon.cluster.wake/route!`'s docstring and TASK-CHAIN section 5 state that a branch write never wakes root and name the form that does (`my.root/run!` for a held task), and a row in an existing `seon.cluster.wake-test` case proves a chain-branch-only write offers no plan wake while the chain fact's installation does; no new wake path. Close this task with no code change to `route!` if the proof row is green on HEAD. Read `seon.cluster.wake/route!`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "root-wakes-a-chain-agent-on-its-chain-branch"
             :plan "S5 run 3 hand act 1 (`docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/s5-run-3.md`): root could not wake a chain agent. `seon.cluster.message/send!` on main answered `:seon.message/undeliverable` with cause `:no-graph`, but the cause was `:seon.message/unknown-recipient` (the chain agent exists only on its chain branch); on the branch connection the message was accepted, but a message carrying `:seon.message/from` is an inside wake (`seon.cluster.wake/inside-wake?`), so `seon.turn/opening-deferred?` kept the agent deferred. The unparseable-reply deferral that needed the wake is gone (`seon.cluster.turn-test/an-unparseable-model-reply-is-delivered-and-the-next-turn-proceeds`), so what remains is two producer defects: (1) `seon.cluster.message/send!` names `:no-graph` for an unknown recipient; it must name `:unknown-recipient` and, for an agent that lives on a chain branch, the branch that holds it; (2) root's message to a chain agent is a fact root writes on that agent's chain branch (root is the only cluster-wide writer; agents transact facts, root reacts), delivered by the branch's own listen route, never a second wake path. Decide by the existing wake declarations whether a message from root is inside (spends the bound) or outside (refills it), and record the decision in TASK-CHAIN section 5. Done when a row in an existing `seon.cluster.wake-test` or `seon.cluster.message-test` case proves root's message to an agent on a chain branch opens that agent's next turn with no hand-offered plan wake, and `send!` on main names the unknown recipient."}]
